      "text": "To determine the measure of ∠ACB, we can use the property that the angles around point B add up to 180°. In this case, we have two adjacent angles: ∠A, which is 93°, and ∠B, which is 130°. \n\nTo find the measure of ∠ACB, we subtract the sum of ∠A and ∠B from 180°:\n\n180° - (93° + 130°) = 180° - 223° = -43°.\n\nHowever, when we calculate subtraction, we might need to consider the direction. Since we subtracted the sum of ∠A and ∠B from 180°, the correct measure of ∠ACB is 180° - 223° = -43°. However, negative angles are not common in geometry problems. The absolute value of the measure of ∠ACB should be taken, since angles are typically measured positively.\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle ∠ACB is 43°.",
